{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs PyPy 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following PyPy 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nThis version of the problem differs from the next one only in the constraint on $$$n$$$.Note that the memory limit in this problem is lower than in others.You have a vertical strip with $$$n$$$ cells, numbered consecutively from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ from top to bottom.You also have a token that is initially placed in cell $$$n$$$. You will move the token up until it arrives at cell $$$1$$$.Let the token be in cell $$$x &gt; 1$$$ at some moment. One shift of the token can have either of the following kinds:   Subtraction: you choose an integer $$$y$$$ between $$$1$$$ and $$$x-1$$$, inclusive, and move the token from cell $$$x$$$ to cell $$$x - y$$$.  Floored division: you choose an integer $$$z$$$ between $$$2$$$ and $$$x$$$, inclusive, and move the token from cell $$$x$$$ to cell $$$\\lfloor \\frac{x}{z} \\rfloor$$$ ($$$x$$$ divided by $$$z$$$ rounded down). Find the number of ways to move the token from cell $$$n$$$ to cell $$$1$$$ using one or more shifts, and print it modulo $$$m$$$. Note that if there are several ways to move the token from one cell to another in one shift, all these ways are considered distinct (check example explanation for a better understanding).\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the PyPy 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO, IOBase\n\nBUFSIZE = 8192\n\nclass FastIO(IOBase):\n    newlines = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, file):\n        self._fd = file.fileno()\n        self.buffer = BytesIO()\n        self.writable = \"x\" in file.mode or \"r\" not in file.mode\n        self.write = self.buffer.write if self.writable else None\n\n    def read(self):\n        while True:\n            b = os.read(self._fd, max(os.fstat(self._fd).st_size, BUFSIZE))\n            if not b:\n                break\n            ptr = self.buffer.tell()\n            self.buffer.seek(0, 2), self.buffer.write(b), self.buffer.seek(ptr)\n        self.newlines = 0\n        return self.buffer.read()\n\n    def readline(self):\n        while self.newlines == 0:\n            b = os.read(self._fd, max(os.fstat(self._fd).st_size, BUFSIZE))\n            self.newlines = b.count(b\"\\n\") + (not b)\n            ptr = self.buffer.tell()\n            self.buffer.seek(0, 2), self.buffer.write(b), self.buffer.seek(ptr)\n        self.newlines -= 1\n        return self.buffer.readline()\n\n    def flush(self):\n        if self.writable:\n            os.write(self._fd, self.buffer.getvalue())\n            self.buffer.truncate(0), self.buffer.seek(0)\n\nclass IOWrapper(IOBase):\n    def __init__(self, file):\n        self.buffer = FastIO(file)\n        self.flush = self.buffer.flush\n        self.writable = self.buffer.writable\n        self.write = lambda s: self.buffer.write(s.encode(\"ascii\"))\n        self.read = lambda: self.buffer.read().decode(\"ascii\")\n        self.readline = lambda: self.buffer.readline().decode(\"ascii\")\n\nsys.stdin, sys.stdout = IOWrapper(sys.stdin), IOWrapper(sys.stdout)\ninput = lambda: sys.stdin.readline().rstrip(\"\\r\\n\")\n\ndef compute():\n    n, m = map(int, input().split())\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    s = [0] * (n + 2)\n    dp[n] = 1\n    s[n] = 1\n    \n    for idx in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        dp[idx] = s[idx + 1]\n        k = 2\n        while idx * k <= n:\n            limit = (idx + 1) * k - 1\n            if limit <= n:\n                dp[idx] += s[k * idx] - s[(idx + 1) * k]\n            else:\n                dp[idx] += s[k * idx]\n            k += 1\n        s[idx] += s[idx + 1] + dp[idx]\n    \n    print(dp[1] % m)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    compute()\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom io import BytesIO, IOBase\n\nBUFSIZE = 8192\n\nclass FastIO(IOBase):\n    newlines = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, file):\n        self._fd = file.fileno()\n        self.buffer = BytesIO()\n        self.writable = \"x\" in file.mode or \"r\" not in file.mode\n        self.write = self.buffer.write if self.writable else None\n\n    def read(self):\n        while True:\n            b = os.read(self._fd, max(os.fstat(self._fd).st_size, BUFSIZE))\n            if not b:\n                break\n            ptr = self.buffer.tell()\n            self.buffer.seek(0, 2), self.buffer.write(b), self.buffer.seek(ptr)\n        self.newlines = 0\n        return self.buffer.read()\n\n    def readline(self):\n        while self.newlines == 0:\n            b = os.read(self._fd, max(os.fstat(self._fd).st_size, BUFSIZE))\n            self.newlines = b.count(b\"\\n\") + (not b)\n            ptr = self.buffer.tell()\n            self.buffer.seek(0, 2), self.buffer.write(b), self.buffer.seek(ptr)\n        self.newlines -= 1\n        return self.buffer.readline()\n\n    def flush(self):\n        if self.writable:\n            os.write(self._fd, self.buffer.getvalue())\n            self.buffer.truncate(0), self.buffer.seek(0)\n\nclass IOWrapper(IOBase):\n    def __init__(self, file):\n        self.buffer = FastIO(file)\n        self.flush = self.buffer.flush\n        self.writable = self.buffer.writable\n        self.write = lambda s: self.buffer.write(s.encode(\"ascii\"))\n        self.read = lambda: self.buffer.read().decode(\"ascii\")\n        self.readline = lambda: self.buffer.readline().decode(\"ascii\")\n\nsys.stdin, sys.stdout = IOWrapper(sys.stdin), IOWrapper(sys.stdout)\ninput = lambda: sys.stdin.readline().rstrip(\"\\r\\n\")\n\ndef compute():\n    n, m = map(int, input().split())\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    s = [0] * (n + 2)\n    dp[n] = 1\n    s[n] = 1\n    \n    for idx in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        # Subtraction moves: from idx to any cell from 1 to idx-1\n        dp[idx] = s[idx + 1] % m\n        \n        # Floored division moves: from idx to floor(idx/z) where 2 <= z <= idx\n        k = 2\n        while k <= idx:\n            # For each z = k, we go to cell floor(idx/k)\n            # Count how many z give the same result: from floor(idx/(k+1))+1 to floor(idx/k)\n            next_k = idx // (idx // k) + 1\n            # Sum dp values for all positions we can reach via division\n            target = idx // k\n            # Add dp[target] multiplied by number of z values that give this target\n            count = (idx // k) - (idx // next_k)\n            dp[idx] = (dp[idx] + count * dp[target]) % m\n            k = next_k\n        \n        # Update prefix sum s[idx] = s[idx+1] + dp[idx]\n        s[idx] = (s[idx + 1] + dp[idx]) % m\n    \n    print(dp[1] % m)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    compute()\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.00010907054820563644, -2.7418097943154862e-06, 0.0, -0.002026410773396492, -0.4288265109062195, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -7.486063259420916e-05, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.00021455370006151497, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.3351351299206726e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-0